From 63f4d80973aa8fc91eaf1123c4a8a26af9b3f88f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <assistant@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 23:16:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Final redesign: characters + private knowledge + ambiguous
 evidence, no secrets/objectives

Applied directly to all existing built-in stories (the 12 original ones,
plus gala_toast added in the previous pass) rather than adding another
new story. Play-testing found secrets/objectives unnecessary complexity
on top of the free-discussion + evidence model from the last phase.

CORE MODEL: name, profession, publicIdentity, privateKnowledge, guilty.
No secret. No objective. No crime objective.

StoryCharacter.java:
- New lean 5-arg constructor (name, profession, publicIdentity,
  knowledge, guilty) is now the only one used anywhere in current
  content. The old 9-arg constructor is kept solely so any already-saved
  custom story's data still loads without crashing -- never used for new
  content, never displayed regardless of story.

Story.java (all 12 original stories + gala_toast regenerated):
- Every character's secret field is now empty. Every knowledge field
  is real, distinct private knowledge -- reused as-is for the ~75
  characters whose knowledge was already neutral/ambiguous, and rewritten
  for the 25 guilty characters across 12 stories whose knowledge used to
  self-incriminate (e.g. old text like "...because you're the one who
  erased it" -> a neutral observation anyone could plausibly know).
  Evidence (InvestigationRound) was already ambiguous from the previous
  phase and needed no changes -- verified by a test that greps every
  evidence string for every character's name (word-boundary aware, to
  avoid false hits like the name "Nader" inside the unrelated word
  "rare").
- Added a solution field for the final reveal: who was guilty, what
  they did, how, which evidence pointed to them, and how other
  characters' private knowledge fit together -- composed from the
  original secret/crimeObjective authoring, moved from "shown to the
  guilty player mid-game" to "shown to everyone at the end." Empty for
  fixed-cast stories, which keep using customEnding (unchanged fallback
  chain, see reveal() below).

MainActivity.java:
- roleReveal(): now shows only public identity + private knowledge (plus
  the pre-existing detective bonus panel). No secret/statement/objective/
  crimeObjective panel, unconditionally, for every story.
- reveal(): prefers story.solution when present, falls back to
  customEnding, then the old generic strings -- old saved custom stories
  (no solution field) are unaffected.
- Custom Story Builder: character step now collects only name,
  profession, publicIdentity, privateKnowledge, guilty. Evidence step
  relabeled and made fully optional (was previously mandatory).
- Untouched: navigation(), exitStoryDialog(), exitAppDialog(), home(),
  onBackPressed handling, RTL setup.

CustomStoryStore.java:
- load() migrates old secret values into knowledge when a saved story
  predates this redesign (no knowledge key present), and simply never
  reads objective/crimeObjective/statement -- verified with a dedicated
  migration-logic test (old-format data, current-format data, and a
  story missing the evidence key entirely all load without crashing).
- save() writes only the current schema (name, profession,
  publicIdentity, knowledge, guilty) -- no dead secret/objective/
  statement/crimeObjective keys for newly created stories.

GameManagerTest.java: rewritten character-shape assertions for the new
architecture, added evidence-ambiguity/knowledge-uniqueness/final-reveal
coverage, and a 4-12 player count sweep. 14/14 tests pass.

Not touched: AndroidManifest.xml, colors.xml, strings.xml (the
your_knowledge string was already exactly the label this needed),
the launcher icon resources, GameManager.java, InvestigationRound.java,
InvestigationData.java, Player.java, Clue.java, StoryIntroduction.java.
No Question Cards, no killer missions/actions, no detective/special roles
beyond what already existed, no branching endings, no online features.
